WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.457 --> 00:00:09.040
SPEAKER: In this
video, you will learn

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.040
how to use Cloud Logging to
determine what caused a Google

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:15.310
Compute Engine instance to
unexpectedly reboot or shut

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:16.390
down.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:18.760
With a few exceptions,
most activities

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.860
related to a virtual
machine's lifecycle

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:22.990
are automatically
added to Cloud Logging

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:24.790
in the form of an audit log.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.490
Audit logs are easiest
way to get insights

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.690
about a VM's activities.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.510
When a VM reboots or
shuts down unexpectedly,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.130
try doing the following.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.530
Select the VM that
shut down or rebooted.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:41.640
In this example, the VM named My
Instance shut down and rebooted

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.540
unexpectedly several times
in the last seven days.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.880
On the log section of
the VM details page,

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.220
click Cloud Logging.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.470
Cloud Logging opens with
a pre-built query that

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.450
filters for the instance ID.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:57.970
Narrow down your data set
using additional filters.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:00.270
Select the log
name dropdown menu

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:04.410
and look for cloud audit
category, or search for audit.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.350
Select the activity
and system event logs.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.140
Activity logs describe
changes made to GCP resources,

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.170
such as compute instances.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.480
System event logs
describe events

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.180
generated by Google systems.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.700
Narrow down the search to
an approximate timeframe

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.220
when the VM reboot or
termination occurred.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.100
In this example, we know the
shutdown occurred sometime

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.170
in the last seven days.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.710
Each cloud audit log entry
displays a compute instance's

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.710
label that describes
the method associated

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.900
with a particular
instance and which

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.720
principal, otherwise known
as a user or service account,

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.030
was associated with that method.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.580
The following log
entries are activities.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.090
Audited operations that cannot
be executed instantly will run

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.210
asynchronously, and therefore
may appear twice in the Cloud

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.500
Logging results.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.560
The first entry represents
the request itself,

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.320
and the second entry represents
the outcome of the request,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.530
such as a success
or an error message.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:05.670
For example, this log entry
illustrates that, on April 4,

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.699
a stop instance method
was issued to My Instance

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:12.840
by the principal
user@example.com.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.140
Then, on April 7, a
reset instance method

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.810
was issued to the same
instance by the service account

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.990
schedulerbot@example.com.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:25.350
The following log entries
are system events.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.800
System events will always
show system@google.com

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.440
listed as the principal
because these events aren't

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.940
driven by direct user action.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.450
This log entry illustrates
that, on April 8,

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:39.550
My Instance was shut down
by the operating system.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:42.360
Note that operating system
reboots are not tracked

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.070
by the cloud audit logs.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.230
If the audit logs
do not adequately

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.750
explain why an
instance was rebooted,

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.750
then it was most likely caused
by an operating system reboot.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.390
In that case, consider
investigating the operating

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.470
system logs for further clues.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.710
Finally, these two log
entries logged on April 9

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.920
indicate that the machine was
shut down due to a host error

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.960
and was automatically restarted.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.820
Occasionally, there
might be problems

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.640
with the physical machine
that hosts the VM.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.620
This is referred
to as a host error

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.830
and is indicated by the
compute.instances.hostError

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:21.250
method, which means that
the VM was terminated.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.010
Some examples of events
that might cause host errors

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.470
are physical
hardware malfunctions

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.480
or a natural
disaster that impacts

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.300
the location of the
Google Cloud data center.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.000
After a host error, the VM is
set to restart automatically,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.315
which is indicated by
the compute.instance

00:03:37.315 --> 00:03:39.610
s.automaticRestart method.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.680
To protect your
applications and services

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.230
from these potentially
disruptive system events,

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:46.630
Google has shared several
best practices that

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:49.360
explain how you can design
robust systems in Google

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.480
Cloud, use patterns for
scalable, resilient apps,

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.970
and encourages using
managed instance groups

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.830
when appropriate.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:59.850
In addition, Compute Engine
also offers live migration

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:03.240
to keep your instances running
during a maintenance event.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.700
There are two methods which
might appear after a Compute

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.290
Engine maintenance event.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.750
The compute.instance
s.migrateOnHostMaintenance

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.850
method indicates that
the Compute Engine

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.820
used a live migration
to keep your VM running

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.680
during a maintenance event.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.350
Compute Engine live migrates
your running instances

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.209
to another host
in the same zone,

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:24.390
instead of requiring
your VMs to be rebooted.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:26.220
This allows Google to
perform maintenance

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.860
that is integral to keeping
infrastructure protected

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:32.310
and reliable without
interrupting any of your VMs.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:34.680
Live migrations are
enabled by default,

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.750
except for VMs with GPUs.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:40.020
But this can be disabled
under the availability policy

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.700
of the particular instance.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:45.030
The compute.instance
s.terminateOnHostMaintenance

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:48.570
method indicates that a live
migration was disabled or is

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.370
not supported on
that particular VM,

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:54.390
so the VM was terminated
during the maintenance event.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.150
For more information on how
to troubleshoot VM shutdowns

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.090
or reboots and a list of
other common methods found

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.670
in Cloud Logging, check out
the official troubleshooting

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:05.890
documentation with the link
below in the description.

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:06.750
Thanks for watching.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:10.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

